{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>You shall not remove your neighbor's boundary-mark.</b> This is a prohibition against changing the boundary of the division that the leaders made when they divided the land among the tribes or among individuals. Therefore Scripture says, <i>which the first ones marked out</i> — these being Elazar the priest, Yehoshua son of Nun, and the leaders of the tribes. This is why it mentions <i>in your inheritance that you inherit</i>, etc. The reason for this commandment is that a person might not consider it theft at all, thinking: my portion that they gave me is not equal to my companion's portion, for those who divided erred — or he may harbor in his heart contempt for the lots. Therefore Scripture commanded here that no one may contest that division or alter the boundaries at all, neither secretly nor openly. This commandment is elucidated by what Scripture commanded (Numbers 26:56), <i>according to the lot shall the inheritance be divided, between the many and the few</i>, and by what it says (Numbers 34:17), <i>These are the names of the men who shall apportion the land to you</i>, etc. According to the opinion of our Sages (Sifrei Shofetim 188), that this is a prohibition against uprooting one's fellow's boundary in the field, for which one transgresses two negative commandments, the sense of <i>which the first ones marked out</i> would be: do not alter the old boundary secretly and say, 'it has always been here.' Scripture warned in the present tense, because a new boundary is conspicuous and known, and no one alters it. And our Sages themselves also raised the question (ibid.): from where do we learn that one who uproots tribal boundaries transgresses a negative commandment? Scripture says, <i>you shall not remove your neighbor's boundary-mark</i> — and this is in truth the plain sense of the verse."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:51:59.203330920Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}